1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,290
No matter where you drag this point along the circle, the geometric invariant never changes! With one auxiliary line, watch the hidden circle theorem unlock!

2
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:25,186
Step 1. A clean title slide appears on a white background, displaying the Korean text '팔각형 매듭' above the English subtitle 'Octagonal Knot,' quietly introducing the geometric figure we are about to construct.

3
00:00:25,186 --> 00:00:37,922
Step 2. A completely blank white canvas is shown with only small 'Start' and 'End' buttons at the top, building anticipation for the transformation that is about to unfold.

4
00:00:37,922 --> 00:00:51,906
Step 3. A single bold blue rectangular strip appears on the white background, oriented diagonally from upper-right to lower-left — this is the very first protagonist of our octagonal knot.

5
00:00:51,906 --> 00:01:07,642
Step 4. A pink rectangular strip crosses over the blue one at a diagonal angle, and as the two strips intersect, the relationship between parallel lines and alternate interior angles becomes visually apparent.

6
00:01:07,642 --> 00:01:22,442
Step 5. A light purple rectangular strip joins the blue and pink ones, weaving between them so that three strips now interlock at different angles, and the outline of a three-dimensional knot begins to emerge.

7
00:01:22,442 --> 00:01:36,834
Step 6. A cyan rectangular strip is added to the three existing ones, and as all four strips mesh together, the vertices of an octagon start to take shape at the center of their intersection.

8
00:01:36,834 --> 00:01:51,802
Step 7. An olive-green rectangular strip is layered onto the four existing strips, creating a complex five-way weave in which congruent triangles appear repeatedly at every point where the strip edges meet.

9
00:01:51,802 --> 00:02:06,386
Step 8. A broad bright-cyan face fills in the center of the five-strip structure, making the core of the knot clearly visible as six colored faces lock together to form each side of the emerging octagon.

10
00:02:06,386 --> 00:02:22,290
Step 9. The entire knot rotates slightly to the right while the tail of the blue strip extends long toward the lower-left, and at this angle it becomes visually clear that all interior angles are equal and all sides are the same length.

11
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:40,474
Step 10. The knot rotates once more so that a light sky-blue face turns toward the viewer and the blue strip's tail hangs prominently to the lower-left, illustrating how each strip supports the others in a structure that embodies the geometric meaning of the Pythagorean theorem.

12
00:02:40,474 --> 00:02:57,674
Step 11. The tail of the blue strip disappears off-screen, leaving only the completed octagonal knot centered on the canvas, its six interlocking colored faces forming a perfect regular octagon through the principles of congruence and similarity.

13
00:02:57,674 --> 00:03:22,386
Step 12. The final slide displays the GitHub URL 'https://min7014.github.io/math20250204001.html' along with an invitation to paste the link into a browser to watch the full animated version of the octagonal knot construction.
